{
  "note": "Appendix A of the MCS6500 Hardware Manual, which prints the address bus, the data bus and the read write line for every cycle of every addressing mode. It is the only manufacturer statement of NMOS cycle behaviour this project has found, so it is the rung one source for what the part puts on its bus and when. Each shape below carries the manual's own address expressions rather than a paraphrase, so a reader can hold the page beside the record. Where the part drives something other than what the page prints, the row carries partDrives and the disagreement is in divergences.json.",
  "document": "manufacturer/mcs6500-hardware-manual-1976.pdf",
  "section": "Appendix A, Summary of Single Cycle Execution",
  "readOn": "2026-08-21",
  "readHow": "Every page of the appendix read as printed, at pdf pages 168 to 180, rather than through the extracted text, which drops the read write column on four tables and garbles two others.",
  "opening": {
    "quote": "Note that the processor will not stop in any cycle where R/W is a 0 (write cycle). Instead, it will go right into the next read cycle and stop there. For this reason, some instructions may appear to be shorter than indicated here.",
    "why": "The appendix is written for someone single cycling a real board, so it warns that a write cycle cannot be stopped in. That is a property of the stop mechanism rather than of the instruction, and it is why a bus trace taken by hand can be shorter than these tables. A model has no such limitation and reproduces every cycle."
  },
